    "set": [
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "e3e363d1dd294ccb8e6ff3b76d99bc33"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "## Methods and interpretation\n\n1985–86 through 1988–89; within-school random assignment. The outcome is average subject percentile rank on the Stanford Achievement Test: reading, word recognition and mathematics. Each subject ranked against pooled regular and regular/aide students within grade; three subject percentiles averaged.\n\nThese are reduced-form initial class-type assignment effects from Table V, column 7. Controls include school fixed effects, student race, student gender, free-lunch status; teacher covariates are excluded. Standard errors are robust, allowing within-class correlated residuals. N is the grade-level analysis sample across all three arms, not the number assigned to the intervention. Follow-up is end of the indicated grade.\n\nGrade estimates share participants and comparison groups; they are not eight independent trials.\n\nAttrition, class switching and first-grade re-randomization complicate interpretation.\n\nRegular classes had some part-time aide access in grades 1–3.\n\nColumn 7 excludes teacher covariates; column 8 is a different specification.\n\nPercentile-point effects cannot be ranked directly against Saga standard-deviation effects.\n\nThe 2003 economic model's 0.20 SD assumption is not this column's outcome unit.\n\nThe 2003 economic analysis is a separate model, not a matched observed-cost series for these eight estimates. No cost-effectiveness ranking or SD conversion is implied.\n\nSource: [Experimental Estimates of Education Production Functions](https://hceconomics.uchicago.edu/sites/default/files/events/Krueger_1999_QJE_v114_n2.pdf), Table V, pp. 512–513; methods pp. 507–511; Table V notes p. 513.",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "unset": []
  },
